WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.640
Big budgets used to be exclusive to major&nbsp;
Hollywood films. But that was before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.080
current Golden Age of Television started&nbsp;
in the late 1990s. TV has never been this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.160
good — or this pricey. Here are some of the most&nbsp;
expensive scenes ever shot for the small screen.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.600
At $217 million, "The Pacific" was the&nbsp;
most expensive miniseries ever made when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.720
it was released in 2010. Its depiction of the&nbsp;
Peleliu beach landings in World War 2 alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.080
cost $5 million, a figure that becomes even more&nbsp;
staggering when you read The Hollywood Reporter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.920
invoice breakdown. Some 300 actors worked on the&nbsp;
four-day shoot. The leads' daily rate was $700,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:45.280
stunt extras' $150, and regular extras' $70.&nbsp;
This was economical according to the producers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.720
who avoided big stars to offset&nbsp;
the show's real money burners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.480
which included uniforms, visual effects, and a&nbsp;
whole arsenal of guns, explosives, and tanks.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.520
Then there was the CGI. Even simple effects,&nbsp;
such as adding a muzzle flash to a gunshot, cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.440
about $700. More complicated adjustments, such as&nbsp;
removing or "masking" features in the image, ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:11.360
about $40,000 per shot. Three-and-a-half-seconds&nbsp;
of a landing craft, for example, cost almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.880
$30,000. And these are just the post production&nbsp;
expenses. Each standard take during principal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.760
photography cost $13,500. The wide shots, of which&nbsp;
there were four per day, could add up to $70,000.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:29.120
Spectacle costs money. That's why the pilot&nbsp;
episode for "Lost," which famously depicts a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:36.720
plane crash, cost ABC between $10 and 14 million&nbsp;
– the most expensive pilot in history at the time.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.600
The scene was constructed in multiple locations.&nbsp;
It began aboard a mocked up plane interior in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:47.440
California sound stage, where director J.J. Abrams&nbsp;
used a skillful blend of practical ingenuity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.080
and CGI to capture the horrifying panic&nbsp;
as the aircraft drops out of the sky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.640
The CGI takes over as we see the plane&nbsp;
break apart and scatter across the ocean.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.440
Some additional disturbing moments were filmed on&nbsp;
the coast of Oahu, Hawaii. It was here that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.480
most expensive prop was used: a decommissioned&nbsp;
airliner. The passenger plane was bought in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.760
Mojave Desert and then transported to Hawaii,&nbsp;
where it was staged in catastrophic fashion.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.840
Fortunately, ABC's investment would&nbsp;
reap major dividends, "Lost" became one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.240
of the biggest shows of the 2000s – and,&nbsp;
occasionally, one of its most confusing.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.320
"Alright dude, we're from the future."

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.920
"Game of Thrones" is a big show. A very&nbsp;
big show. Everything about it is massive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.800
including the budget. Metro reports that the&nbsp;
notorious eighth season cost $90 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.520
which means $15 million for each of&nbsp;
the season's six episodes. Of these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.320
it's likely that the Battle&nbsp;
of Winterfell cost the most.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.240
All of the pricey ingredients are here –&nbsp;
a sweeping cast with established fame and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.040
hefty salaries, scores of extras decked&nbsp;
in unique costumes, towering set design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.080
and seamless integration of CGI. However,&nbsp;
this huge battle split opinion among viewers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.560
Many bemoaned how dimly lit the cinematography&nbsp;
was, complaining that they had to adjust the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.920
brightness on their TVs because&nbsp;
it was simply too dark to watch.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:03.360
"I can't see."

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.280
"That's your problem. Not mine."

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.960
Other viewers loved it, with a headline&nbsp;
at GamesRadar quoting a claim that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.680
"the most intense episode in television history."

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:17.680
The $18 million pilot of "Boardwalk Empire" is&nbsp;
effectively one big piece of world-building that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.560
displays the show's glitz and glamour, including&nbsp;
the lavish boardwalk set that cost $5 million.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:24.240
"That's a lot."

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:25.600
"That's quite a lot."

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.640
The boardwalk set was staged in&nbsp;
Greenpoint, Brooklyn and took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.960
months to build. In an interview with the&nbsp;
Chicago Tribune, set designer Bob Shaw said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.760
that it was the biggest outdoor set built&nbsp;
in New York City in almost a hundred years.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.120
Then there were the small details.&nbsp;
The storefronts were based closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.440
on the Atlantic City boardwalk circa&nbsp;
1920. That's just one part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.200
exhaustive research that went into the&nbsp;
project, which covered everything from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.600
key moments of World War I and prohibition&nbsp;
to the wider popular culture of the era.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.840
Budgets tend to bulge when warfare and,&nbsp;
especially, fire-breathing dragons are involved.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.000
(dragon looking up, purr, squeak!)

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.240
"The Crown" has neither of those things,&nbsp;
but 70 years of royal history doesn't come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.840
cheap. In fact, "The Crown" is one of&nbsp;
the most expensive shows ever made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.560
with the first season costing $130 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.520
The priciest scene in the 10-episode run is&nbsp;
Queen Elizabeth II's ["the Second's"] wedding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:23.200
It features a replica dress that, according&nbsp;
to Harper's Bazaar, cost $37,000 to make.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.280
This stand-out piece of wardrobe was just&nbsp;
one part of the show's ornate grandeur.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.640
In a conversation on the red&nbsp;
carpet, actor Claire Foy said…

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:35.760
"Every single penny was spent on screen. [...] All&nbsp;
the locations are appropriate but extraordinary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.160
The costumes were hand made and designed. Also&nbsp;
we were shooting in London. [...] It's so rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.080
that you're able to shoot in London. [...]&nbsp;
We shut down the Mall for an entire morning!"

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.320
Matt Smith, who played Prince Phillip, added…

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.480
"We had great costumes, great locations [...]&nbsp;
so I think all the money went on screen."

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.120
"Sense8" was always going to be an expensive&nbsp;
show because its eight main characters are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.680
from every corner of the earth. According to&nbsp;
Wired, this globetrotting extravaganza was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:07.120
costing Netflix $9 million per episode when it was&nbsp;
cancelled in June 2017. Of the show's 24 episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.040
Season 2's Christmas special was likely&nbsp;
the most expensive — in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.480
an underwater scene that took the&nbsp;
cast and crew all the way to Malta.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.840
Filming underwater is far more&nbsp;
complex than filming on dry land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.720
and it often requires up to three times as many&nbsp;
crew members. Then there are creative concerns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.120
namely the complications affecting light and&nbsp;
color, which means waiting for the opportune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.000
time period on the open sea or using elaborate&nbsp;
lighting equipment if working in a tank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:38.160
Either way, it's a matter of crew, time, and&nbsp;
technology, all of which costs plenty of money.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.960
"Jack Ryan" is one of Amazon's proudest exhibits.&nbsp;
It announced the company's intention to spend big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.480
money on big drama –- $8 million per episode,&nbsp;
to be exact. Every action scene is an explosive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.440
testament to Amazon's deep pockets. Pinpointing&nbsp;
a certain scene isn't easy because there are so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:59.040
many to choose from, but the subway shootout in&nbsp;
Season 1, Episode 8 is definitely a highlight.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.280
As with so much of contemporary TV,&nbsp;
the lines between reality and CGI are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.160
blurred as Jack Ryan edges down the subway&nbsp;
tunnel, gun in hand. After a brief shootout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.680
a train barrels along the tracks, obliterating one&nbsp;
of the men Ryan's just shot. And it's exactly that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.080
type of CGI-driven moment that can take&nbsp;
a big bite out of an episode's budget.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:20.320
"It was totally worth it."

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.960
Most fans like to see their protagonists kick&nbsp;
ass, especially when they're dealing with zombies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:30.800
Depicting this is usually a rather modest affair,&nbsp;
at least in the scheme of mega-budget TV. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.760
which "Walking Dead" scene took the biggest chunk&nbsp;
out of the show's budget? Well, Season 7's ninth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:41.600
episode saw prices go up when it featured two old&nbsp;
cars, a line of wire, and a whole lotta walkers.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.840
The bulk of the cost was likely incurred&nbsp;
during post-production — processing all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.840
of those CGI flying limbs does not&nbsp;
come cheap. Even the walkers' moans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.240
and groans were added by audio specialists;&nbsp;
the undead extras are asked to remain silent.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.440
As usual, this scene was an amplification of a&nbsp;
budget that was already full of expensive details.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:03.040
For example, Yahoo reports that&nbsp;
AMC paid the families of Senoia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:07.920
Georgia — where the majority of the series is&nbsp;
filmed — $400 a month to compensate for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:15.840
hassle of production and to discourage locals&nbsp;
from sharing any spoilers they might have seen.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.346
"Shhhhh…”

00:07:17.346 --> 00:07:19.200
[dramatic music]

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.280
When "Friends" started in 1994, the&nbsp;
budget was so tight that producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.480
had to use the "bottle episode"&nbsp;
format, which required the crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.640
to use as few sets as possible and not&nbsp;
even think of having special guests.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:32.800
Well, as everyone on the face of the earth knows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.320
"Friends" would become one of the biggest&nbsp;
shows in the history of American television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:39.760
earning Warner Brothers a yearly&nbsp;
syndication revenue of $1 billion.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:45.520
This success saw the modestly-staged show end&nbsp;
up costing a pretty penny for NBC. By the eighth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:51.440
season, each main cast member was earning&nbsp;
$750,000 for every 30-minute episode. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:57.520
$25,000 a minute. So, when Brad Pitt turned up for&nbsp;
Thanksgiving dinner, it couldn't have been cheap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.000
Still, it's a no-brainer when the choice is&nbsp;
between money and an unforgettable episode.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.400
"What's more important? Your friends or Money?"

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.960
(cross talk) "Friends." "Money!" "Friends."

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:12.400
Just like with "Lost" back in 2004, showrunner&nbsp;
J.J. Abrams wanted "Fringe" to make a strong first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:17.760
impression. Unfortunately for Fox executives,&nbsp;
a sequence that included scores of extras,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:23.200
face-melting special effects, and a custom-built&nbsp;
set would contribute to a $10 million budget.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:27.520
The cost breakdown would have shared much in&nbsp;
common with "Lost" — a mocked-up plane interior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.720
a soundstage, and detailed CGI. Only this&nbsp;
time, there was a strong serving of gory makeup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:37.920
applied to about a dozen extras aboard the doomed&nbsp;
plane. "Fringe," like other entries on this list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:43.040
would see the budget fall after its pilot,&nbsp;
plateauing at a rate of $4 million per episode.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:49.360
Yet another case of pilot extravagance, the&nbsp;
costumes, period sets, and star power of "Vinyl"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:54.240
is said to have cost $30 million. However,&nbsp;
despite the collaboration of Mick Jagger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.960
and Martin Scorsese, "Vinyl" landed&nbsp;
with neither critics nor audiences.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:03.920
So why did it cost so much money? Well, much&nbsp;
like 70s rock, too much is never enough.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.320
"That's what I'm talking about."

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:10.400
The pilot episode was effectively one big&nbsp;
scene crammed with everything viewers could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.760
expect from the rest of the season. In the case of&nbsp;
"Vinyl," this manifested in a large ensemble cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:20.320
a sweeping wardrobe of authentic '70s&nbsp;
garb, and a catalog of licensed music.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:26.560
The pilot's $30 million price tag was just&nbsp;
33% of the first season's $100 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:29.280
which would ultimately sink the&nbsp;
show before it could take off.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:31.245
The Big Bang Theory: Wedding vows with Mark Hamill

00:09:31.245 --> 00:09:36.560
"The Big Bang Theory" had become a massive show by&nbsp;
its 11th season. Its 250 + episodes brimmed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:41.760
the affectionate geekiness that fans around the&nbsp;
world identified with. So for the season's final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.720
episode, which featured the marriage of Amy and&nbsp;
Sheldon, the show needed an extra special guest.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.840
That guest, of course, was Mark&nbsp;
Hamill, the iconic "Star Wars" actor.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:53.360
"It’s Mark Hamill."

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:57.680
Hamill was the perfect choice to officiate the&nbsp;
wedding of two super geeks and he also brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.400
some extra star power to the show. Although,&nbsp;
this wedding episode already boasted a bevy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:09.040
of high-profile cameos, including Kathy Bates,&nbsp;
Jerry O'Connell, Laurie Metcalf, Wil Wheaton, plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:14.480
many more. And that's, basically, how a simple&nbsp;
sitcom can cost $9 million for a single episode

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:19.040
"Rome" is another lavish production&nbsp;
that was overwhelmed by its budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.240
In many ways, "Rome" set the stage for&nbsp;
"Game of Thrones," which would reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:26.800
a far bigger audience using a similar&nbsp;
brew of power, politics, and violence.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.400
The budgetary peak for "Rome" came with&nbsp;
the depiction of the Battle of Philippi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:36.160
which occurred in 42 B.C. during the Liberator's&nbsp;
civil war. It's a huge scene that mixes dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.800
of extras clad in full suits of armor&nbsp;
and carrying a variety of weapons. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:46.080
everything we see is supplemented by CGI that&nbsp;
multiplies the scene's scope many times over.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:49.520
TV had never been staged like&nbsp;
this before — a towering blend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.800
of sword-and-sandal grandeur mixed&nbsp;
with modern grit and violence.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:57.680
"Game of Thrones" is a show that's too big&nbsp;
to mention just once. Of all the chaos and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:01.680
majesty that we witness, the dragon&nbsp;
attack in Season 7's fourth episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:05.280
is perhaps the most awesome — and&nbsp;
the most expensive. The execution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:09.280
of this remarkable sequence was&nbsp;
detailed in a short HBO documentary.

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:12.800
When director of photography Rob&nbsp;
McLachlan learned about the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:14.960
he had an understandable response...

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:18.160
"Oh boy, that is going to be a lot of work."

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:22.800
McLachlan's team operated at least four&nbsp;
cameras, sometimes seven or eight. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.360
also used three different tracking vehicles to&nbsp;
capture the huge Dothraki charges, which were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:32.240
also filmed on cameras hoisted on industrial&nbsp;
cranes. Then there were the helicopter drones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:37.120
which were supplied by a third party to add&nbsp;
further dynamic shots to this complex sequence.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:41.520
The zenith of the episode occurs when the dragon&nbsp;
focuses his deadly attack on the loot train,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:45.920
which we see from several perspectives. The&nbsp;
practical effects team worked tirelessly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:49.600
create a seamless line of explosions&nbsp;
that would not only look authentic but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.600
also push an already epic depiction of&nbsp;
a dragon in battle to the next level.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.880
Check out one of our newest&nbsp;
videos right here! Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:00.560
even more Looper videos about your&nbsp;
favorite TV shows are coming soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

